to france now where the government has  proposed a sweeping new security bill  following a series of recent terror  attacks among other things the  new law would make it a criminal offence  to publish  images of police officers with intent to  cause them harm  it sparked an outcry among journalist  unions and free speech campaigners  sonia fanika reports from paris  french documentary the monopoly of  violence is a hard-hitting examination  of police brutality in france  released earlier this year the film  focuses on the yellow west protests  as well as allegations of police abuse  in the suburbs  filmmaker david dufren relied on footage  provided by journalists  as well as ordinary citizens posting on  social media  but making a film like this may not be  possible in future with the french  government proposing a new security law  part of which cracks down on the  broadcast and sharing of images of  police officers  different says the law erodes basic  freedoms  and puts france on a slippery slope save  your laws  police violence has existed in france  for decades especially in the suburbs  today it's well documented it's filmed  social media allows for the easy  broadcasting of these videos  what lawmakers want to do is stop the  circulation of these images  and at that point you're not in a  democracy anymore  the french parliament has been debating  the controversial law  one of its provisions stipulates jail  time and a fine of more than fifty  thousand dollars for anyone  broadcasting or sharing identifiable  images of police officers  with the intention of harming them  [Applause]  it sparked large demonstrations here in  paris  as well as in several other french  cities journalists unions and rights  groups are up in the arms saying that  recording officers in action  is essential to being able to condemn  and curb the actions  of violent officers many believe the law  which applies to both journalists and  citizens  will lead to a sense of impunity of  police add demonstrations  that often turn violent i know friends  who are really scared to go to  demonstrations because of the violence  the rubber bullets  and the tear gas used by police and the  one thing that protects us  is taking a video the law wants to  protect police officers so that they can  hit people without consequences  journalists have been hit and injured by  police especially in the last couple of  years  and until now the police version of  events could be questioned  thanks to images and videos  campaigners here fear that the new  security law could have a chilling  effect on press freedom  they say that if citizens and  journalists are dissuaded from filming  the police  it may make it harder to hold certain  officers to account  the french government however says the  law is necessary to protect police  officers  in the line of duty the country's  largest police union which had been  pushing for the law  agrees with government david olivier  revedi  says indiscriminate posting of images of  officers  especially on social media has led to a  dangerous backlash  today several of our colleagues are  victims of harassment  they or their families are threatened in  certain low-income neighborhoods you'd  even find walls with the names of police  officers  saying that their daughter or their wife  would be raped it's completely  unacceptable  the new law can protect and put a stop  to this  recent terror attacks and growing unrest  are protests have prompted the french  government to take a harder line on  security  but the proposed law is only expected to  deepen divisions  and fan further mistrust between the  police and the public  ltw sonia falakka filed this report and  is joining us now from paris  sonia why is this law being proposed  right now  well i think uh the current atmosphere  in france uh certainly plays a role you  know the recent terror attacks  uh growing unrest demonstrations all  that has certainly prompted the  government to take a tougher line  um on security but there are also  commentators here who say you know  domestic politics also plays a role  they say you know president macron's  toughest stance in security is an  attempt to appeal to voters on the right  ahead of the next presidential election  which takes place less than a year and a  half away  but i think more broadly the french  government has defended this bill and  said that  you know restrictions and images on uh  of police officers are necessary in  order to protect officers as well as  their families  uh from being targeted in online  campaigns  well just to make this clear this bill  would make it a crime to circulate an  image of a police officer  with the intention that they be harmed  but who decides  if the intention was to harm someone  well again that that is exactly the  problem you know rights groups here and  that includes france's own human rights  watchdog have said that this particular  clause in the bill article 24  uh that you mentioned this is very very  problematic because it's vague it's open  to interpretation and very difficult to  prove because the question is really  how courts would determine whether you  know images were posted  with the with the um intent to cause  harm so i think this is problematic and  there are fears that this could be uh  you know open to abuse and ultimately  this may make it harder for journalists  to cover protests and demonstrations  but to be clear i mean this is not only  about journalists you know the bill  applies to citizens too and there are  experts here in france  who say that you know video footage  which is very often filmed by passers-by  and and posted on social media before  the mainstream media reports on it  as has also led in the past to to cases  of police brutality coming to the fore  and there are concerns here that this uh  may not be possible anymore once  the new law is passed well mps are  scheduled to vote  on this bill as a whole on tuesday it  will then go to the senate that's  frank's upper house  what can we expect will it pass  well um you know last week we saw very  heated debate in the french parliament  of this bill there were lawmakers who  were divided on it  um especially mps from you know left and  centrist parties  who were really concerned about the  impact of this particular clause on  press freedom but that article was  eventually approved  after a statement was added that it  would guarantee um  you know kind of the right to inform but  i think all this has not been really  enough to to ease the concerns of  campaigners here and i think  we are likely to see you know further  protests against this proposed  legislation  in paris as well as in other french  cities  dw sol sonia farnika reporting from  paris thank you sonia thank you  you
 

